## Solution

**Problem.** Determine the cardinality of $\prod_{n\in\mathbb{N}}(n+1)$.

**Interpretation.** Here $(n+1)$ denotes the set $\{0,1,\dots,n\}$ (the finite ordinal with $n+1$ elements). Taking $\mathbb{N}=\{0,1,2,\dots\}$, the product
$$\prod_{n\in\mathbb{N}}(n+1)$$
is the set of all sequences $(a_0,a_1,a_2,\dots)$ with $a_n\in\{0,1,\dots,n\}$ for every $n$ — equivalently, the set of functions $f:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ satisfying $f(n)\le n$ for all $n$.

---

**Upper bound.** Since $\{0,1,\dots,n\}\subseteq\mathbb{N}$ for every $n$, we have a natural inclusion
$$\prod_{n\in\mathbb{N}}(n+1)\;\subseteq\;\prod_{n\in\mathbb{N}}\mathbb{N}\;=\;\mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}.$$
Now $|\mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}|=\aleph_0^{\aleph_0}$. Using the standard cardinal arithmetic:
$$2^{\aleph_0}\;\le\;\aleph_0^{\aleph_0}\;\le\;(2^{\aleph_0})^{\aleph_0}=2^{\aleph_0\cdot\aleph_0}=2^{\aleph_0},$$
so $|\mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}|=2^{\aleph_0}=\mathfrak{c}$. Hence
$$\left|\prod_{n\in\mathbb{N}}(n+1)\right|\;\le\;\mathfrak{c}.$$

**Lower bound.** We inject $2^{\mathbb{N}}$ into the product. Note $a_0\in\{0\}$ is forced (the $0$-th factor is the singleton $\{0\}$). For each binary sequence $(b_1,b_2,b_3,\dots)\in\{0,1\}^{\mathbb{N}}$, define
$$\Phi(b_1,b_2,\dots)=(0,\,b_1,\,b_2,\,b_3,\dots),$$
i.e. $a_0=0$ and $a_n=b_n$ for $n\ge 1$. Since $b_n\in\{0,1\}\subseteq\{0,1,\dots,n\}$ for every $n\ge 1$, this sequence lies in $\prod_{n\in\mathbb{N}}(n+1)$. The map $\Phi$ is clearly injective (it just prepends a $0$). Therefore
$$\left|\prod_{n\in\mathbb{N}}(n+1)\right|\;\ge\;|2^{\mathbb{N}}|=2^{\aleph_0}=\mathfrak{c}.$$

**Conclusion.** Combining the two bounds,
$$\boxed{\left|\prod_{n\in\mathbb{N}}(n+1)\right|=2^{\aleph_0}=\mathfrak{c}.}$$

The cardinality is that of the continuum. (Note: the convention on whether $\mathbb{N}$ starts at $0$ or $1$ does not affect the answer — it only changes whether the leading factor is the singleton $\{0\}$ or the two-element set $\{0,1\}$, which does not alter the cardinality of the infinite product.)

